// Phase 13 Plan 01 Task 1 -- the tracer defect: a caller borrows its own
// parameter, calls a callee that genuinely reads through its own parameter
// (usesParam == true, D-08-02's deriveFunctionUsesParam), then `take`s the
// owner while that call's own operation is still the recorded LAST USE of
// the shared loan (the BACKWARD direction, D-08-08 / TestInterprocedural
// LivenessTwinPatternB's synthetic shape, reproduced here as a real, source-
// level program). This is exactly the swap-fixable shape (D-13-09.1): the
// `take` currently happens BEFORE the call that is the loan's true last
// use, and relocating it to AFTER the call resolves the conflict with no
// observable behavior change (D-13-11 -- `take` is a pure compile-time
// ownership transfer, never a Drop).
//
// Expected: `check` refuses with exactly one check.interprocedural_loan_
// liveness diagnostic, whose repair (move_after_interprocedural_loan)
// swaps the `result = user(borrowed)` and `delivered = take buffer`
// statements; applying that repair re-checks clean (verified both by this
// plan's end-to-end CLI check and by
// TestRepairDriverFixesInterproceduralLoanLivenessSinglePass).
//
// `user` cannot merely forward its parameter to its own OpReturn (D-08-01's
// zero-hop exemption would make usesParam false and this fixture would
// admit with zero diagnostics, per TestInterproceduralLivenessTwinPatternB's
// own "callee does not use its parameter" branch) -- it must genuinely
// route through an intermediate operation, here `take borrowed`, before
// returning the moved place.

module phase13.derivation

export {
  fn escort
}

fn user(borrowed: Buffer) -> Buffer {
  let moved = take borrowed
  moved
}

fn escort(buffer: Buffer) -> Buffer {
  let borrowed = borrow buffer
  let delivered = take buffer
  let result = user(borrowed)
  result
}
